 ## Overview
-Fourier Domain Approximate Shading is a method for approximating diffuse shading in the Fourier domain by expressing shading terms (linear, quadratic, cubic) as convolutions of the volume's Fourier transform $F$ and frequency vectors [S1]. It solves the problem of applying spatial lighting models to volume data rendered via Fourier Volume Rendering (FVR) without requiring repeated 3D Fourier transforms of the shaded volume for constant light sources [S1]. Unlike [Spherical Harmonics](6052-spherical-harmonics.md), which allows efficient relighting, this method is efficient only for constant light sources because changing the light direction requires recomputing expensive full-volume convolutions [S1].
+Fourier Domain Approximate Shading approximates diffuse shading in the Fourier domain by expressing shading terms (linear, quadratic, cubic) as convolutions of the volume's Fourier transform $F$ and frequency vectors [S1]. It solves the problem of applying spatial lighting models to volume data rendered via Fourier Volume Rendering without requiring repeated 3D Fourier transforms of the shaded volume for constant light sources [S1]. Unlike [Spherical Harmonics](6052-spherical-harmonics.md), which supports efficient relighting, this method is efficient only for constant light sources because changing the light direction requires recomputing expensive full-volume convolutions [S1].
 
